        "geology": "The catchments are underlain by basaltic lavas, which overlie Clarens Sandstone. Three post-Karoo dolerite dykes, each 3 m wide, run through the research area but they are assumed to exert no hydrological influence.",
        "hydrology": "Approximately 49 % of the rainfall is converted to streamflow, with two thirds of the streamflow yield occurring during the four months from January to April.",
        "soils": "The soils of the catchments are acidic, highly leached and are classified as Lateritic Red and Yellow earths, grading into heavy black soils (Katspruit and Champagne) in the saturated zones and along the stream banks. The mean soil depth in the catchments is approximately 0.80 m, with an A-horizon depth of 0.20 m. However the mean soil depth of catchment VI is 0.50 m with an A-horizon depth of 0.25 m. The depth of the soil profile is at least 1.5 m with Hutton and Griffin forms occurring frequently on the gentler slopes of the catchments.",
        "vegetation": "Primarily C4 grasslands, occasional  with forest patches, wetlands and C3 grasslands at higher altitude."
      },
